Evaluation of Swallowing (FEES)** is indicated. Management involves dietary modifications such as **thickened liquids** (nectar or honey consistency to slow the bolus transit time) and **altered food textures** (e.g., pureed or mechanical soft) along with chin-tuck maneuvers during swallowing.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-old female is brought to the clinic by her family due to concern for weight loss. The family reports that she has lost **32 lbs** (14.5 kg) over the past **3 months**, which was completely unintentional. This represents a **18.0% decline** in her baseline body weight of 178 lbs. She has no specific symptoms other than mild fatigue. Which of the following defines the clinical threshold for significant unintentional weight loss in an elderly patient, and what is the most appropriate initial diagnostic step?', '["Loss of \u226520% of body weight in 1 year; place a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) tube immediately to prevent further decline", "Loss of \u22655% of body weight in 6 months or \u226510% in 12 months; obtain a detailed history and physical examination, nutritional assessment (e.g., Mini Nutritional Assessment), and baseline laboratory testing (CBC, CMP, TSH, CRP, urinalysis)", "Loss of \u226510% of body weight in 1 month; initiate total parenteral nutrition (TPN) and admit to the hospital for observation", "Loss of \u226515% of body weight in 3 months; immediately order a whole-body CT scan and schedule an upper and lower endoscopy", "Loss of \u22655 lbs regardless of baseline weight or duration; prescribe Megestrol acetate to stimulate appetite before initiating any diagnostic workup"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient meets the clinical criteria for **significant unintentional weight loss**, which is defined as an **involuntary weight loss of ≥5% of baseline body weight in 6 months or ≥10% in 12 months**. Unintentional weight loss in older adults is associated with increased morbidity, disability, and mortality. The initial diagnostic approach should always begin with a **comprehensive history, physical examination, and detailed nutritional assessment** (such as the Mini Nutritional Assessment, MNA), along with a search for common organic, psychosocial, or medication-related causes (e.g., the ''''9 Ds'''' of geriatric weight loss). 